  "contracts": {
    "thm-tdmath-continuous-ideal-gas-block-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. For $E \\in \\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$, define\n\n$$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(E) := \\int_X \\nu(E_x)\\,d\\mu = \\int_Y \\mu(E^y)\\,d\\nu.$$\n\nThe two displayed integrals are well-defined by\n[[prop-sigma-finite-section-measure-functions-are-measurable]] and equal by\n[[thm-iterated-section-measures-agree-on-product-measurable-sets]]. This is the\n**product measure** of $\\mu$ and $\\nu$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt\\pi.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Lebesgue measurable\n$f : V \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-of-square-roots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then every\n$a \\in F$ with $a \\ge 0$ has a **unique** $s \\in F$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^2 = a$;\nwe write $s = \\sqrt{a}$. Consequently the positive elements of $F$ are exactly the\nnonzero squares: $x > 0$ if and only if $x = y^2$ for some $y \\neq 0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-integral-logarithm-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $L:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $u, v : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at\nevery point of $[a,b]$ as functions on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]). Suppose\n$u'$ and $v'$ are integrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then $uv'$\nand $u'v$ are integrable and\n\n$$\\int_a^b u\\,v' \\;=\\; u(b)v(b) - u(a)v(a) \\;-\\; \\int_a^b u'\\,v .$$\n\n**The integrability of $u'$ and $v'$ is a hypothesis, not a formality.** Without\nit the two integrals in the display need not exist at all, and the identity is\nthen not false but ill-formed; that is\nthe false statement that deletes it on the companion\npage. The hypothesis is automatic when $u$ and $v$ are continuously\ndifferentiable, since a continuous function on $[a,b]$ is integrable\n([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]).\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and every real $a>0$,\n$$\\frac{x^m}{\\exp(ax)}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(x\\to+\\infty).$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The integrand is nonnegative, so [F3] factors the position and momentum integrals: $$Z_N=\\frac1{N!h^{dN}}\\int_{\\Lambda_N^N}\\int_{\\mathbb R^{dN}} e^{-\\beta\\sum_i|p_i|^2/(2m)}\\,dp\\,dq =\\frac{V_N^N}{N!h^{dN}}\\left(\\int_\\mathbb R e^{-\\beta p^2/(2m)}\\,dp\\right)^{dN}.$$ By [F4]–[F5] the one-coordinate integral is $\\sqrt{2\\pi m/\\beta}$, proving the stated exact formula with $\\lambda_\\beta=h\\sqrt{\\beta/(2\\pi m)}$. The dimensions are consistent: $\\lambda_\\beta$ is a length, so $V_N^N/\\lambda_\\beta^{dN}$ and $Z_N$ are dimensionless. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $G$ have the density in [A2]. By [F4]–[F5], it is normalized. Apply [F7] on $[-R,R]$ first with $u(x)=x$, $v'(x)=xe^{-x^2/2}$, and then with $u(x)=x^3$, $v'(x)=xe^{-x^2/2}$. In each case $v(x)=-e^{-x^2/2}$. Letting $R\\to\\infty$, the endpoint terms vanish by [F8], giving $\\mathbb E G^2=1$ and $\\mathbb E G^4=3$. Thus $\\mathbb E(G^2-1)=0$ and $\\mathbb E(G^2-1)^2=2$. Product measure and [F3] make distinct coordinates independent, so expanding the square and factoring cross integrals gives $$\\mathbb E(A_N-1)^2=M^{-2}\\sum_{a=1}^M2=2/M.$$ For any $\\eta>0$, $\\eta^2\\mathbf1_{\\{|A_N-1|>\\eta\\}}\\le(A_N-1)^2$, so integration proves $P(|A_N-1|>\\eta)\\le2/(M\\eta^2)\\to0$. Hence $A_N\\to1$ in probability. [A2, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F8, algebra]",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F3",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Monotonicity of $\\log$ from [F6] gives $\\int_1^N\\log x\\,dx\\le\\log N!\\le\\int_1^{N+1}\\log x\\,dx$, hence $\\log N!=N\\log N-N+O(\\log(N+1))$. Substituting the exact $Z_N$ yields $$N^{-1}\\log Z_N=\\log(V_N/N)+1-d\\log\\lambda_\\beta+o(1)\\to-\\log\\rho+1-d\\log\\lambda_\\beta.$$ Multiplication by $-\\beta^{-1}$ proves the per-particle limit; multiplying by $N/V_N\\to\\rho$ proves the per-volume limit. [A1, F6, step 1.1, algebra]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Since $A_N>0$ almost surely, the shell-prepared variables are defined. Algebra gives $$\\sum_{a=1}^{dN}\\frac{(P_a^{\\rm sh})^2}{2m} =\\frac1{2\\beta A_N}\\sum_aG_a^2=\\frac{M}{2\\beta}=\\frac{dN}{2\\beta}.$$ This exact energy constraint holds on every outcome where $A_N>0$, an event of probability one since the joint Gaussian density assigns zero mass to the origin. [A2, step 1.2]",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "For fixed $r$, the scalar multiplier $A_N^{-1/2}$ tends to $1$ in probability by continuity at $1$ and step 1.2. Therefore $(P_1^{\\rm sh},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm sh})-(P_1^{\\rm can},\\ldots,P_r^{\\rm can})$ tends to zero in probability: restrict first to the event $\\sum_{a=1}^rG_a^2\\le R^2$, where its norm is at most $\\sqrt{m/\\beta}R|A_N^{-1/2}-1|$, and then bound the complement by $P(\\sum_{a=1}^rG_a^2>R^2)\\le r/R^2$ using the second moment. For bounded continuous $\\psi$, choose $R$ so this tail is small, use uniform continuity of $\\psi$ on the compact ball of radius $2\\sqrt{m/\\beta}R$, and then use convergence in probability of the difference. On the complement the expectation difference is at most $2\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$. Sending first $N\\to\\infty$ and then $R\\to\\infty$ proves the claimed expectation limit. [A2, F1, F3, step 1.2]",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If positions are included, choose them independently and uniformly in the same cube under both preparations. After coordinatewise rescaling by the side length, every fixed position block already has the same uniform law in both models and is independent of the momentum vector. The same bounded-continuous test argument as in 2.3, first on compact sets and then on their small tails, proves the joint fixed-block limit. This does not change the energy computation because the Hamiltonian has no position dependence. [A1, A2, F1, step 2.3] ∎",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.3",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1"
          ]
        }
      ],
