    "thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **symplectic form** on a smooth manifold $M$ is a smooth two-form $\\omega$\nsuch that\n\n1. $d\\omega=0$, and\n2. $(T_pM,\\omega_p)$ is a [[def-symplectic-vector-space|symplectic vector\n   space]] for every $p\\in M$.\n\nThe pair $(M,\\omega)$ is a **symplectic manifold**. Thus both closedness and\npointwise nondegeneracy are required. A zero-dimensional manifold with its\nzero two-form satisfies the definition.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $H\\in C^\\infty(M)$, its **Hamiltonian vector field** is the vector field\n$X_H$ determined by the library sign convention\n\n$$\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH.$$\n\nA vector field $X$ is **Hamiltonian** if $\\iota_X\\omega=dH$ for some smooth\nfunction $H$; such an $H$ is a **Hamiltonian function for $X$**. A function and\nits vector field are distinct data, and completeness of $X_H$ is not assumed.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-symplectic-manifolds-have-a-canonical-orientation-and-volume-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ is symplectic, then\n$\\mu_\\omega=\\omega^n/n!$ is a nowhere-zero volume form. Its positive ray gives\nthe canonical **symplectic orientation** of $M$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-liouville-volume-preservation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On a $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold, every Hamiltonian local flow\npreserves the Liouville volume form $\\Omega=\\omega^n/n!$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space. A measurable self-map $T:X\\to X$ is **measure preserving** if $\\mu(T^{-1}E)=\\mu(E)$ for every $E\\in\\mathcal A$. The quadruple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu,T)$ is a **measure-preserving system**; it is a probability system if $\\mu(X)=1$. Here $T^{-1}E=\\{x:T(x)\\in E\\}$ denotes an inverse image, whether or not $T$ is invertible. Neither completeness nor finiteness is implicit. The measure-space and measurable-map conventions are [[def-measure-space]] and [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-integrals-are-invariant-under-measure-preserving-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $T$ preserves $\\mu$ and $f:X\\to[0,\\infty]$ is measurable, then $\\int f\\circ T\\,d\\mu=\\int f\\,d\\mu$, allowing infinity. If $f$ is integrable real or complex valued, $f\\circ T$ is integrable and the same equality holds. Conversely, for a measurable self-map, equality for every measurable indicator implies measure preservation.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a measure-preserving system $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu,T)$ with $\\mu(X)<\\infty$, for every $E\\in\\mathcal A$ almost every $x\\in E$ has $T^nx\\in E$ for infinitely many positive integers $n$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure-preserving system is **ergodic for $\\mu$** if each $E\\in\\mathcal I$ has $\\mu(E)=0$ or $\\mu(X\\setminus E)=0$, with $\\mathcal I$ as in [[def-strict-and-mod-null-invariant-sigma-algebras]]. For a probability system this means $\\mu(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$. The definition is relative to the invariant measure; no probability assumption is implicit in the general null/conull formulation.\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-birkhoff-ergodic-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be sigma-finite, let $T$ preserve $\\mu$, and let $f$ be a\nfinite-valued real- or complex-valued measurable representative in\n$\\mathcal L^1(\\mu)$.  Then $A_nf$ converges $\\mu$-almost everywhere to a\nfinite-valued integrable function $f^*$ satisfying\n\n$$f^*\\circ T=f^*\\quad\\mu\\text{-almost everywhere},\\qquad \\lVert f^*\\rVert_1\\leq\\lVert f\\rVert_1.$$\n\nThe a.e. class of $f^*$ depends only on the a.e. class of $f$.  No ergodicity,\nfinite total measure, completeness, or invertibility is assumed.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-birkhoff-ergodic-theorem-for-ergodic-probability-systems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice.  Let $T$ preserve an ergodic measure $\\mu$ with\n$0<\\mu(X)<\\infty$.  For every real- or complex-valued $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n\n$$A_nf\\longrightarrow \\frac1{\\mu(X)}\\int_Xf\\,d\\mu$$\n\nboth $\\mu$-almost everywhere and in $L^1(\\mu)$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $\\omega$ is closed and $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. The Hamiltonian-flow theorem [F2], under its local existence domain, gives $\\phi_t^*\\omega=\\omega$ and hence $\\phi_t^*(\\omega^n/n!)=\\omega^n/n!$. The volume measure defined by this positive form is therefore preserved on each invariant domain; restriction to $R$ gives the measure-preserving system of [F3]. Also $dH(X_H)=\\omega(X_H,X_H)=0$ because a two-form is alternating, so each defined orbit stays on its energy level. This argument grants no flow outside its domain. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, algebra]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "A2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] to $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$. It preserves $\\int p_t\\,dm=\\int p_0\\,dm=1$. The scalar function $u\\mapsto u\\log u$, defined to be $0$ at $u=0$ by [A3], is Borel; its absolute integrability is preserved because the same measure-preserving substitution gives $$\\int_R|p_t\\log p_t|\\,dm=\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty.$$ Applying integral invariance to its positive and negative parts proves $\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm=\\int_Rp_0\\log p_0\\,dm$, hence the stated fine-grained entropy conservation. [A2, A3, F3, F6, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "A3",
            "A2",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The map $T$ on $R$ is measure preserving by 1.1 and [A2]. Its total measure is finite, so [F4] gives infinite returns for almost every point in each measurable $E\\subseteq R$. The conclusion is explicitly almost everywhere and applies to the sampled map at the specified $\\tau$. [A2, F3, F4, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Under the additional probability and ergodicity assumptions in [A2], the hypotheses of [F5] hold for each $f\\in L^1(R,m)$. Birkhoff therefore gives almost-everywhere and $L^1$ convergence to the invariant conditional expectation, and its ergodic corollary identifies that limit with $\\int_R f\\,dm$. If ergodicity is removed, only the invariant conditional expectation conclusion remains. Neither [F2] nor recurrence [F4] supplies ergodicity. [A2, F5, step 1.1] ∎",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F5",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
