    "thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $d\\ge2$. The **elastic collision set** is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}:=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in\\mathbb R^{4d}:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\},$$\nwith its relative Borel sigma-algebra. A collision measure is a nonnegative countably additive measure $\\nu$ on this measurable space. It is symmetric if it is invariant under the exchanges $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(w,v)$, $(v',w')\\leftrightarrow(w',v')$, and $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(v',w')$. These invariances are hypotheses on $\\nu$, not consequences of energy and momentum conservation.\n\nFor a positive function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$, write $Q(f)$ for a collision operator when the following **weak collision identity** holds for each test function $\\varphi$ used:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\varphi(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(f(v')f(w')-f(v)f(w))(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w'))\\,d\\nu,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This identity is the adopted mathematical collision model. Molecular chaos or a microscopic derivation of it is not part of the definition.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. For $E \\in \\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$, define\n\n$$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(E) := \\int_X \\nu(E_x)\\,d\\mu = \\int_Y \\mu(E^y)\\,d\\nu.$$\n\nThe two displayed integrals are well-defined by\n[[prop-sigma-finite-section-measure-functions-are-measurable]] and equal by\n[[thm-iterated-section-measures-agree-on-product-measurable-sets]]. This is the\n**product measure** of $\\mu$ and $\\nu$.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a fixed time, write $\\ell=\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})$. By [F3]–[F4], dominated differentiation gives $$\\frac{dH}{dt}=\\int_\\Omega\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}(1+\\ell)\\,\\partial_t f\\,dv\\,dr.$$ Substitute the transport equation. The spatial contribution is $$-\\int_\\Omega\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}(1+\\ell)\\,v\\cdot\\nabla_r f\\,dv\\,dr =-\\int_\\Omega\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}v\\cdot\\nabla_r(f\\ell)\\,dv\\,dr.$$ For each fixed $v$, the integral of each coordinate derivative over the periodic rectangle is zero by the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus and opposite-face identification; absolute integrability in [A2] permits Fubini [F2]. Thus the transport contribution vanishes. [A1, A2, F2, F3, F4, algebra]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The weak identity with test $1$ has zero bracket on every elastic collision, so $\\int Q(f)\\,dv=0$ at each $r,t$. Consequently the collision contribution from $(1+\\ell)Q(f)$ equals that from $\\ell Q(f)$. Apply the weak identity with $\\varphi=\\ell$ at fixed $(r,t)$. The sum $\\ell(v)+\\ell(w)-\\ell(v')-\\ell(w')$ is $\\log A-\\log B$, since the constant reference terms cancel. Hence $$\\int \\ell(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(B-A)(\\log A-\\log B)\\,d\\nu.$$ Integrate in $r$; the absolute convergence in [A2] justifies the rearrangement by [F2]. This is the asserted derivative identity. [A1, A2, F1, F2, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For positive $A,B$, strict increase of $\\log$ [F5] makes $(A-B)(\\log A-\\log B)\\ge0$, with equality exactly when $A=B$. The derivative in 1.2 is therefore nonpositive. If its nonnegative production integral is zero, the integrand vanishes almost everywhere: for each $n$, the set on which it is at least $1/n$ must have measure zero, since otherwise its integral is positive. Thus $A=B$ for $\\nu$-almost every collision at almost every $r$. The converse follows by substitution in the derivative identity. [A2, F2, F5, step 2.1, algebra]",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Under [A3], fix an $r$ for which collision balance holds $\\nu$-almost everywhere. The function $(v,w,v',w')\\mapsto A-B$ is continuous. If it were nonzero at an allowed collision, it would remain nonzero on a relative open neighborhood; full support of $\\nu$ gives that neighborhood positive measure, contradicting balance. Hence balance holds for every elastic collision. The set of such $r$ has full Lebesgue measure and is dense in $\\Omega$; continuity in $r$ extends the identity to any limit point when needed. [A1, A3, F1, step 3.1, algebra]",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A3",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Fix such an $r$ and put $g(v)=\\log(f(r,v,t)/f_{\\rm ref})$. For any $a,u\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and any orthogonal rotation $R$, the pairs $(a+u,a-u)$ and $(a+Ru,a-Ru)$ have the same total momentum $2a$ and total squared speed $2|a|^2+2|u|^2$. Step 4.1 therefore gives $$g(a+u)+g(a-u)=g(a+Ru)+g(a-Ru).$$ For unit vectors $\\theta,\\eta$, put $u=s\\theta$ and $R\\theta=\\eta$. The second-order Taylor expansion at $a$ gives $$g(a+s\\theta)+g(a-s\\theta)=2g(a)+s^2\\theta^\\top D^2g(a)\\theta+o(s^2).$$ Equating the expansion for $\\eta$, dividing by $s^2$ and sending $s\\to0$ shows that the quadratic form of the symmetric Hessian is constant on the unit sphere. Testing coordinate vectors makes all diagonal entries equal; testing $(e_i+e_j)/\\sqrt2$ makes each off-diagonal entry zero. Thus $D^2g(a)=\\lambda(a)I$. [A1, A3, step 4.1, algebra]",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "A1",
            "A3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\partial_{ij}g=0$ for $i\\ne j$, the fundamental theorem of calculus along coordinate segments shows $\\partial_i g(v)$ depends only on $v_i$. Write it as $G_i(v_i)$. The diagonal Hessian equalities give $G_i'(v_i)=G_j'(v_j)$ for every pair of independent real coordinates $v_i,v_j$. Because $d\\ge2$, varying these coordinates independently forces all these derivatives to be one constant $\\lambda$. Integrating each first derivative gives $g(v)=\\alpha+b\\cdot v+\\lambda|v|^2/2$. [A1, step 5.1, algebra]",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "If $\\lambda>0$, $e^{g(v)}$ grows quadratically in every sufficiently large direction and is not integrable. If $\\lambda=0$, $e^{\\alpha+b\\cdot v}$ is constant when $b=0$ and grows on a half-line when $b\\ne0$, so it is not integrable over $\\mathbb R^d$. Thus integrability forces $\\lambda<0$. Completing the square writes $f(r,v,t)=C(r,t)\\exp[-c(r,t)|v-u(r,t)|^2]$ for $C,c>0$, the Maxwellian velocity form. Conversely, when $g=\\alpha+b\\cdot v+\\lambda|v|^2/2$, the sum $g(v)+g(w)$ is unchanged by every collision because total momentum and total squared speed are conserved; hence $A=B$ and the production is zero. This equality branch concerns the collision term only; a spatially varying Maxwellian may still evolve under transport. [A1, A3, F1, step 6.1, algebra] ∎",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A3",
            "F1",
            "step 6.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
