          "status": "Current draft supplier carrier; exact interface inspected; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $d\\ge2$. The **elastic collision set** is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}:=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in\\mathbb R^{4d}:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\},$$\nwith its relative Borel sigma-algebra. A collision measure is a nonnegative countably additive measure $\\nu$ on this measurable space. It is symmetric if it is invariant under the exchanges $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(w,v)$, $(v',w')\\leftrightarrow(w',v')$, and $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(v',w')$. These invariances are hypotheses on $\\nu$, not consequences of energy and momentum conservation.\n\nFor a positive function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$, write $Q(f)$ for a collision operator when the following **weak collision identity** holds for each test function $\\varphi$ used:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\varphi(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(f(v')f(w')-f(v)f(w))(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w'))\\,d\\nu,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This identity is the adopted mathematical collision model. Molecular chaos or a microscopic derivation of it is not part of the definition.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft definition carrier is present; its interface was inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 2.1, 4.1, 7.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "carrier": "items/def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": ""
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. For $E \\in \\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$, define\n\n$$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(E) := \\int_X \\nu(E_x)\\,d\\mu = \\int_Y \\mu(E^y)\\,d\\nu.$$\n\nThe two displayed integrals are well-defined by\n[[prop-sigma-finite-section-measure-functions-are-measurable]] and equal by\n[[thm-iterated-section-measures-agree-on-product-measurable-sets]]. This is the\n**product measure** of $\\mu$ and $\\nu$.\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published definition; no proof is required.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-chain-rule",
          "carrier": "items/thm-chain-rule.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/cor-mean-value-theorem.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "carrier": "items/thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step(s) 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "carrier": "items/thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical supplier proof is present in the imported read-only snapshot.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "Current published mathematical supplier; exact interface inspected in its read-only snapshot.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact statement excerpt is retained above. The consumer uses precisely the hypotheses and conventions recorded in its linked Facts & Assumptions entry.",
