    "thm-tdmath-regular-energy-shell-measure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $O\\subset\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge2$, be open, let $H\\in C^2(O,\\mathbb R)$, and let $J\\subset\\mathbb R$ be open. An energy $E\\in J$ is **regular** if $\\nabla H(x)\\ne0$ for every $x\\in\\Sigma_E:=H^{-1}(\\{E\\})$. By the implicit-function theorem, a neighborhood of each point of a nonempty regular level is a graph $x_i=h(z)$.\n\nIn a graph chart, use the local area density $\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla h(z)|^2}\\,dz$, where $dz$ is $(m-1)$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. A **graph partition** on $\\Sigma_E$ is a locally finite graph-chart cover with nonnegative smooth functions $\\chi_a$ supported in their respective charts and summing to one on the level. Whenever such a partition is supplied, define the candidate weighted shell integral by\n$$\\mu_E(g):=\\sum_a\\int g(z,h_a(z))\\chi_a(z,h_a(z))|\\partial_iH(z,h_a(z))|^{-1}\\,dz$$\nfor nonnegative Borel $g$. Its independence from the chosen chart partition, and its equality with $\\int_{\\Sigma_E}g|\\nabla H|^{-1}dA_E$, are proved in the theorem on this page. If $0<\\mu_E(1)<\\infty$, the **microcanonical shell probability** is $\\widehat\\mu_E:=\\mu_E/\\mu_E(1)$.\n\nThis is an explicit chart-based definition, not a formal delta distribution $\\delta(H-E)$. Critical levels are excluded.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m+n}$ be open, and let\n$F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$.  Suppose $(a,b)\\in U$, $F(a,b)=0$, and the\n**partial derivative in the second block**\n\n$$D_yF(a,b):\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad D_yF(a,b)v:=DF(a,b)(0,v),$$\n\nis invertible.  Put similarly $D_xF(a,b)u:=DF(a,b)(u,0)$.  Then there are open\nneighbourhoods $P$ of $a$ and $Q$ of $b$, and a unique $C^1$ map\n$\\varphi:P\\to Q$, such that\n\n$$F(x,y)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=\\varphi(x)\\qquad((x,y)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nAfter shrinking $P,Q$ if necessary, $D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))$ is invertible and\n\n$$D\\varphi(x)=-D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))^{-1}D_xF(x,\\varphi(x)).$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Lebesgue measurable\n$f : V \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(M,\\omega)$ be a smooth $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold and $H\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, with Hamiltonian field defined by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. Wherever its local flow $\\phi_t$ is defined, it preserves the symplectic form and the volume measure $m$ determined by $\\omega^n/n!$. No completeness is asserted. For a region $R$ on which a nonzero-time map $T=\\phi_\\tau$ and all its iterates are defined, $T$ is a measure-preserving transformation. If $m(R)<\\infty$, then for every measurable $E\\subseteq R$, almost every point of $E$ returns to $E$ infinitely often under forward iteration.\n\nIf $m(R)=1$ and $T$ is ergodic, then for every $f\\in L^1(R,m)$ the averages $N^{-1}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}f(T^jx)$ converge almost everywhere and in $L^1$ to $\\int_Rf\\,dm$. Without ergodicity, the limit is the invariant conditional expectation supplied by Birkhoff's theorem, not necessarily the space mean. Finally, if each stated time map $\\phi_t$ is a diffeomorphism of a finite invariant region $R$, a density $p_0\\ge0$ with $\\int_Rp_0\\,dm=1$ and $\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty$ transports by $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$ with conserved fine-grained entropy $-k_B\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm$, using $0\\log0=0$. Volume preservation alone does not imply ergodicity or a time-average/ensemble identity.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-distance-to-set-is-lipschitz",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $A \\subseteq X$ be\nnonempty and let $x, y \\in X$. Then\n\n$$|d(x,A) - d(y,A)| \\le d(x,y),$$\n\nwith $d(\\cdot,A)$ the distance to a nonempty set ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\nThus the real-valued function $u \\mapsto d(u,A)$ changes by at most $d(u,v)$\nbetween $u$ and $v$: it is **$1$-Lipschitz**.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-metric-closure-characterisation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $A \\subseteq X$,\nwith closure, derived set and limit points as in\n[[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]]. Then:\n\n1. If $A \\ne \\emptyset$, then $\\overline{A} = \\{\\, x \\in X : d(x,A) = 0 \\,\\}$,\n   where $d(x,A)$ is the distance from a point to a nonempty set\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n2. $\\overline{A} = A \\cup A'$.\n3. $\\overline{A}$ is closed, contains $A$, and is contained in every closed\n   $F \\subseteq X$ with $A \\subseteq F$. So $\\overline{A}$ is the smallest closed\n   superset of $A$, and $A$ is closed if and only if $A = \\overline{A}$.\n\nClaims 2 and 3 hold for every $A$, the empty set included: $\\overline{\\emptyset}$\nis empty because no ball meets $\\emptyset$, and $\\emptyset$ is closed because $X$\nis open ([[def-metric-topology]]). Claim 1 carries the hypothesis $A \\ne \\emptyset$\nbecause $d(x,A)$ is defined only for nonempty $A$\n([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-finite-measure-uniqueness-on-a-pi-system",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P$ be a pi-system on $X$ and let $\\mathcal A=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)$. If finite measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on $(X,\\mathcal A)$ agree on $\\mathcal P$ and satisfy $\\mu(X)=\\nu(X)$, then $\\mu=\\nu$ on $\\mathcal A$.\n\nThe total-mass equality is separate because this library's pi-system convention does not require $X\\in\\mathcal P$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $C$ be a compact energy band containing the support of $f$. At each $x\\in H^{-1}(C)$, some coordinate derivative $\\partial_iH(x)$ is nonzero. By [F2] a neighborhood is parameterized by the remaining coordinates $z$ and energy $E$, with $x_i=h(z,E)$. Compactness gives a finite cover by such charts. Refine the cover by finitely many balls whose closures lie in chart domains and whose smaller concentric balls cover $H^{-1}(C)$. Choose standard smooth bump functions $\\psi_a$ supported in the larger balls and positive on the smaller balls, and let $\\Psi=\\sum_a\\psi_a$. Its minimum on $H^{-1}(C)$ is positive. Choose a smooth scalar cutoff $\\eta$ that is zero below one quarter of this minimum and one above one half of it. Then $\\chi_a=\\eta(\\Psi)\\psi_a/\\Psi$, extended by zero where $\\Psi=0$, is smooth, supported in its chart, and the sum is one near $H^{-1}(C)$. [A1, A2, F2, F4, construct]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In each chart, the map $(z,E)\\mapsto(z,h_a(z,E))$ is injective after shrinking the chart, and its absolute Jacobian is $|\\partial_Eh_a|=|\\partial_iH|^{-1}$. By [F3] and Tonelli [F4], $$\\int_O f(x)\\chi_a(x)\\,dx =\\int_J\\int f(z,h_a(z,E))\\chi_a(z,h_a(z,E))|\\partial_iH|^{-1}\\,dz\\,dE.$$ Implicit differentiation gives $\\partial_{z_j}h_a=-\\partial_jH/\\partial_iH$, so the graph area density is $$\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla_zh_a|^2}\\,dz=\\frac{|\\nabla H|}{|\\partial_iH|}\\,dz.$$ The inner integral is therefore the corresponding chart contribution to the candidate $\\mu_E(f)$ in [F1]. Sum the finitely many charts and use $\\sum_a\\chi_a=1$ on the support to obtain the asserted coarea identity. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "On overlaps, if one graph parameterization is obtained from another by a $C^1$ coordinate change $\\psi$, its Gram matrix is transformed by $G\\mapsto(D\\psi)^\\top G(D\\psi)$. Taking determinants shows the area density changes by $|\\det D\\psi|$, exactly the ordinary chart Jacobian. Implicit differentiation gives $\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla h|^2}/|\\nabla H|=|\\partial_iH|^{-1}$, so the chart contributions define the same measure independent of the partition and equal $\\int_{\\Sigma_E}g|\\nabla H|^{-1}dA_E$. Each shell is a closed subset of compact $H^{-1}(\\{E\\})$, hence compact. A finite collection of graph charts with $\\nabla H$ bounded away from zero on that compact level gives finite area and hence $\\mu_E(1)<\\infty$. If the shell is nonempty, one graph chart contains an open patch; its coordinate domain has positive $(2n-1)$-dimensional measure and the continuous density $|\\nabla H|^{-1}dA_E/dz$ is positive there, so $\\mu_E(1)>0$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix a compact band and a compactly supported continuous test $g$ on it. For a continuous compactly supported scalar function $q$ on $J$, the coarea identity from 1.2 and the Hamiltonian volume/energy invariance [F5] give $$\\int_Jq(E)\\mu_E(g\\circ\\phi_t)\\,dE =\\int_Oq(H(x))g(\\phi_tx)\\,dx =\\int_Oq(H(x))g(x)\\,dx =\\int_Jq(E)\\mu_E(g)\\,dE.$$ The second equality is the change of variables by the volume-preserving diffeomorphism $\\phi_t$; $H\\circ\\phi_t=H$ keeps the energy weight fixed. For each test $g$, both functions of $E$ in the outer integrals are continuous locally: use the finite chart representation in 1.2, compact chart support, the positive lower bound for $|\\nabla H|$, and dominated convergence [F6]. Their difference integrates to zero against every compactly supported continuous $q$. If the continuous difference were nonzero at one energy, it would keep one strict sign on a neighborhood; a nonnegative continuous bump $q$ supported there would make the integral nonzero. Thus $\\mu_E(g\\circ\\phi_t)=\\mu_E(g)$ for every $E$ in the band. [A1, A2, F1, F5, F6, step 2.1]",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Fix a closed subset $F$ of a nonempty shell. If $F=\\varnothing$, both measures assign it zero. Otherwise let $\\chi$ be a continuous compactly supported cutoff equal to one on the compact shell; the same finite-ball bump construction used in 1.1 supplies it. In the ambient band set $g_n(x)=\\chi(x)\\max\\{0,1-n\\,\\operatorname{dist}(x,F)\\}$. By [F7], $g_n$ is continuous, $0\\le g_n\\le1$, and its restriction to $\\Sigma_E$ converges pointwise to $\\mathbf1_F$. Because $\\phi_t$ maps the shell onto itself, $g_n\\circ\\phi_t$ converges there to $\\mathbf1_{\\phi_t^{-1}F}$. Apply 2.1 to each $g_n$ and use dominated convergence [F6] for the finite shell measure from 1.3; this gives $\\mu_E(\\phi_t^{-1}F)=\\mu_E(F)$ for every closed $F\\subseteq\\Sigma_E$. Closed sets form a pi-system generating the shell Borel sigma-algebra, and both $\\mu_E$ and its pushforward have the same finite total mass because $\\phi_t$ maps the shell onto itself. [F5, F6, F7, step 2.2, step 3.1]",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "F6",
            "F5",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "By [F8], agreement on closed sets and equality of total masses imply that $\\mu_E$ equals its pushforward on every Borel subset of $\\Sigma_E$. Divide by $0<\\mu_E(1)<\\infty$ from 1.3. Hence the normalized regular-shell probability is invariant for each such $E$, not merely almost every energy. [F8, step 2.2, step 4.1] ∎",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
