        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-general-trace-gibbs-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\mathcal H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $A$ be self-adjoint with regular Borel projection-valued measure $E$ from [[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]], with $E((-\\infty,a))=0$ for finite $a\\in\\mathbb R$. For each $\\gamma>0$, define the bounded Gibbs operator $K_\\gamma:=\\Phi_E(f_\\gamma)$ using the bounded Borel PVM integral of [[thm-bounded-borel-pvm-integral]], where $f_\\gamma(\\lambda)=e^{-\\gamma\\lambda}$ for $\\lambda\\ge a$ and $f_\\gamma(\\lambda)=e^{-\\gamma a}$ for $\\lambda<a$. Fix $\\beta>0$ and suppose $K_\\beta$ is trace class in the sense of [[def-trace-class-operator]]. Then $K_\\beta$ is injective and has a finite or countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $\\mathcal H$ with $Au_j=E_ju_j$, $E_j\\ge a$, and $K_\\beta u_j=e^{-\\beta E_j}u_j$. Its partition function $Z_\\beta:=\\operatorname{Tr}K_\\beta=\\sum_j e^{-\\beta E_j}$ is positive and finite, and $\\rho_\\beta:=K_\\beta/Z_\\beta$ is positive with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_\\beta=1$. For every $B\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H)$, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_\\beta B)=\\sum_j(e^{-\\beta E_j}/Z_\\beta)\\langle Bu_j,u_j\\rangle$ absolutely. Further, let $J\\subset(0,\\infty)$ be open and suppose that for every compact $C\\subset J$ there is a summable family $a_j\\ge0$ with $(1+|E_j|^2)e^{-\\gamma E_j}\\le a_j$ for all $j$ and $\\gamma\\in C$. Then for each $\\gamma\\in J$, $K_\\gamma$ is trace class with partition function $Z_\\gamma:=\\operatorname{Tr}K_\\gamma=\\sum_j e^{-\\gamma E_j}$. With $p_j(\\gamma):=e^{-\\gamma E_j}/Z_\\gamma$, define $\\bar E_\\gamma:=\\sum_jp_j(\\gamma)E_j$, $\\operatorname{Var}_\\gamma(E):=\\sum_jp_j(\\gamma)(E_j-\\bar E_\\gamma)^2$, and $S_\\gamma:=-\\sum_jp_j(\\gamma)\\log p_j(\\gamma)$. These quantities are finite and satisfy $-\\partial_\\gamma\\log Z_\\gamma=\\bar E_\\gamma$, $\\partial_\\gamma^2\\log Z_\\gamma=\\operatorname{Var}_\\gamma(E)$, and $S_\\gamma=\\log Z_\\gamma+\\gamma\\bar E_\\gamma$ as in [[thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation]]. Countable nonnegative sums use the finite-subsum supremum convention of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]].\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-trace-class-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ and\n$K$ be Hilbert spaces over the same field $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$\n([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a compact operator ([[def-compact-linear-operator]])\nwith zero-padded singular-value sequence $(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]).\n\n**Trace class.** The operator $T$ is **trace class** when\n$$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T):=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)<+\\infty .$$\nThus the series in the zero-based convention of\n[[def-series-and-absolute-convergence-in-a-normed-space]] is formed from the\nexplicit sequence $(s_{m+1}(T))_{m\\in\\mathbb N}$. In that case its **trace\nnorm** is\n$$\\|T\\|_1:=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)\\in[0,+\\infty),$$\nand $\\|T\\|_1$ is also written $\\|T\\|_{\\mathrm{tr}}$. The set of trace-class\noperators $H\\to K$ is written $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$.\n\n**Immediate consequences.** Since $0\\le s_n(T)\\le s_1(T)=\\|T\\|$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]), the terms\nare nonnegative and $\\|T\\|_1\\ge\\|T\\|\\ge0$ whenever $T$ is trace class; the zero\noperator is trace class with $\\|0\\|_1=0$. When $T$ has finite rank $r$ the\nsequence is zero-padded, the series is the finite sum\n$\\sum_{n=1}^r s_n(T)=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}\\lambda\\dim_{\\mathbb F} E_\\lambda(|T|)$ over the finitely\nmany positive eigenvalues of $|T|$ counted with multiplicity ([[def-dimension]]),\nand every finite-rank operator is compact\n([[lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact]]) and therefore trace class; in particular every\noperator with finite-dimensional range and every rank-one operator is trace\nclass. If $T$ has infinite rank then $s_n(T)>0$ for every $n$ and the series\n$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T)$ converges in the summable case. By the necessary\ncondition for convergence of a scalar series ([[lem-nth-term-test]]), a\ntrace-class operator with infinite rank has $s_n(T)\\to0$, hence\nis a norm limit of finite-rank operators\n([[thm-singular-value-decomposition-for-compact-operators]]).\n\n**Choice accounting.** Trace class is defined through the singular values of\n[[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]], whose\nconstruction uses $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through the countable selection of finite\northonormal bases of the eigenspaces of $|T|$ and the spectral theorem; no\nHilbert basis of the ambient space, and no stronger choice, is used here.\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-trace-class-is-a-two-sided-banach-operator-ideal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$, $K$\nand $L$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces ([[def-hilbert-space]]). Then:\n\n1. the trace-class operators $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ ([[def-trace-class-operator]])\n   form a linear subspace of $\\mathcal B(H,K)$ on which $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ is a norm,\n   and\n   $$\\|T\\|\\le\\|T\\|_1\\qquad\\text{for every }T\\in\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$$\n   ([[def-operator-norm]]);\n2. if $T\\in\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ and $A\\in\\mathcal B(K,L)$, $B\\in\\mathcal B(H_0,H)$\n   are bounded linear operators on Hilbert spaces $H_0,L$, then\n   $ATB\\in\\mathcal S_1(H_0,L)$ and\n   $$\\|ATB\\|_1\\le\\|A\\|\\,\\|T\\|_1\\,\\|B\\| ;$$\n3. $(\\mathcal S_1(H,K),\\|\\cdot\\|_1)$ is a Banach space: every\n   $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$-Cauchy sequence in $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ has a limit in\n   $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ to which it converges in $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$\n   ([[def-banach-space]], [[def-norm-and-normed-space]],\n   [[def-metric-space]]).\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-finite-rank-compressions-converge-in-trace-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a separable complex Hilbert space, let\n$T\\in\\mathcal S_1(H)$ be trace class, and let $(P_n)_{n\\ge1}$ be any supplied sequence of\nfinite-rank orthogonal projections on $H$ such that $P_n\\to I$ strongly. Then\n$$\\|P_nTP_n-T\\|_1\\longrightarrow0.$$\nThe projections need not be increasing. For example, the initial projections\nonto the first $n$ vectors of a supplied countable orthonormal basis satisfy\nthe hypothesis.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be a **self-adjoint positive** trace-class operator\n([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]],\n[[def-trace-class-operator]]), so that $\\langle Tx,x\\rangle\\ge0$ for every\n$x\\in H$. Let $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots>0$ be the positive eigenvalues of\n$T$ listed with multiplicity\n([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]) and let\n$(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$ be the zero-padded singular-value sequence\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]). Then\n$s_n(T)=\\lambda_n$ for every $n$, and\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(T)=\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\lambda_n=\\|T\\|_1 ,$$\nwhere the sum is over the positive eigenvalues with multiplicity and the\nequalities are also valid in the finite-rank case (then $\\lambda_n:=0$ for\n$n$ beyond the rank). This is the positive compact self-adjoint case only; it is\n**not** Lidskii's theorem for arbitrary trace-class operators, which is not\nclaimed here.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]). Then:\n\n1. for every **nuclear representation** $Tx=\\sum_j\\langle x,u_j\\rangle v_j$ of\n   $T$ (operator-norm convergence of the partial sums,\n   $\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\|v_j\\|<+\\infty$), the scalar series $\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$\n   converges absolutely and\n   $$\\Bigl|\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle\\Bigr|\\le\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\,\\|v_j\\| ;$$\n2. the sum $\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$ depends only on $T$; denoting it\n   $\\operatorname{tr}(T)$, one has\n   $$\\operatorname{tr}(T)=\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$$\n   for **every** nuclear representation of $T$. This defines $\\operatorname{tr}(T)$\n   without assuming that $H$ has a Hilbert basis;\n3. for every supplied Hilbert basis $E$ of $H$,\n   $\\operatorname{tr}_E(T)=\\operatorname{tr}(T)$\n   ([[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]]);\n4. the trace is linear in the trace-class variable and bounded by the trace\n   norm: for trace-class $S,T$ and scalars $a,b$,\n   $\\operatorname{tr}(aS+bT)=a\\operatorname{tr}(S)+b\\operatorname{tr}(T)$ and\n   $|\\operatorname{tr}(T)|\\le\\|T\\|_1$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cyclicity-of-the-trace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) and let\n$S\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be bounded. Then $ST$ and $TS$ are trace class and\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(ST)=\\operatorname{tr}(TS) .$$\nIf $A,B\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ are Hilbert–Schmidt relative to a supplied Hilbert\nbasis $E$ of $H$ ([[def-hilbert-schmidt-operator]]), then the products $AB$ and\n$BA$ are trace class and\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(AB)=\\operatorname{tr}(BA).$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, with **conjugation** $z\\mapsto\\overline z$\nthe identity on $\\mathbb R$ and complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). A **real** or\n**complex inner-product space** is an $\\mathbb F$-vector space $V$ together with\nan inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb F$ in the sense\nof [[def-inner-product-space]]: for all $u,v,w\\in V$ and $a,b\\in\\mathbb F$,\n\n$$\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle=a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle v,u\\rangle},\\qquad \\langle v,v\\rangle\\ge0,\\quad \\langle v,v\\rangle=0\\iff v=0 .$$\n\n**Conjugate-linearity in the second argument.** Conjugate symmetry together with\nlinearity in the first argument gives, for all $w\\in V$,\n\n$$\\langle w,au+bv\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle}=\\overline{a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle}=\\overline a\\,\\langle w,u\\rangle+\\overline b\\,\\langle w,v\\rangle .$$\n\n**Induced length.** The **induced length**, or inner-product norm, of $v$ is\n\n$$\\|v\\|:=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is exactly the published [[def-inner-product-norm]]: positive definiteness\nmakes $\\langle v,v\\rangle$ a nonnegative real with a unique nonnegative square\nroot, so that $\\|v\\|\\ge0$, $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly for $v=0$, and\n$\\langle v,v\\rangle=\\|v\\|^2$.\n\n**The convention on this page.** Every inner-product space below is real or\ncomplex in this sense, the pairing is linear in the first argument and\nconjugate-linear in the second, and $\\|v\\|$ always denotes the induced length.\nIn the real case conjugation is the identity and $|z|$ is the absolute value of\n$\\mathbb R$; in the complex case they are complex conjugation and the complex\nmodulus. All statements below are therefore written once and read in either\nscalar field.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Each multiplier $e^{-iE_jt/\\hbar}$ has modulus one by [F6]. Thus $U_t$ preserves the norm of finite linear combinations of the complete orthonormal basis; it extends by density to an isometry on the Hilbert space. The similarly defined $U_{-t}$ is its inverse, so $U_t$ is unitary and depends continuously on $t$ on each finite-dimensional energy span. The arguments $E_jt/\\hbar$ are dimensionless. [A1, A2, F6, F7]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], $\\rho_t=U_t\\rho U_t^*$ is trace class. It is positive because $\\langle\\rho_t x,x\\rangle=\\langle\\rho U_t^*x,U_t^*x\\rangle\\ge0$, and cyclicity [F5] gives $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_t=\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$. [A2, F2, F5, F7, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "A2",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Enumerate the distinct energy eigenspaces and let $P_N$ project onto the first $N$ of them, with $P_N=I$ after the list is exhausted if it is finite. Their union spans the eigenbasis, so $P_N\\to I$ strongly. The Hilbert space is separable by the countable complete basis in [A1]. Therefore [F3] gives $\\|P_N\\rho P_N-\\rho\\|_1\\to0$. Unitary invariance of trace norm follows from [F2] in both directions: $\\|U_tRU_t^*\\|_1\\le\\|R\\|_1$ and $R=U_t^*(U_tRU_t^*)U_t$ gives the reverse inequality. Consequently $$\\|U_tP_N\\rho P_NU_t^*-\\rho_t\\|_1=\\|P_N\\rho P_N-\\rho\\|_1$$ uniformly in $t$. [A1, A2, F2, F3, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F2",
            "A2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $N$, the matrix of $U_tP_N\\rho P_NU_t^*$ on its finite-dimensional range has continuous entries, hence is continuous in trace norm. Step 2.2 makes $\\rho_t$ a uniform trace-norm limit on compact time intervals of these continuous maps; therefore $t\\mapsto\\rho_t$ is trace-norm continuous. On $[0,T]$ it is uniformly continuous. Riemann sums for a uniformly continuous Banach-valued function are Cauchy: two sums are compared on a common refinement and their difference is bounded by $T$ times the modulus of continuity at the larger mesh. Completeness in [F2] supplies the trace-class integral and its usual norm bound. [F2, step 2.2]",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.2",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "In the eigenbasis, the $(i,j)$ matrix entry of $U_tP_N\\rho P_NU_t^*$ is $\\langle\\rho u_j,u_i\\rangle e^{-i(E_j-E_i)t/\\hbar}$. Its time-average factor is $1$ if $E_i=E_j$; if $E_i\\ne E_j$, direct integration using [F6] gives absolute value at most $2\\hbar/(T|E_j-E_i|)$, which tends to zero. Since the compression is finite dimensional, entrywise convergence implies trace-norm convergence. Hence the finite compressed average tends to $\\sum_{E\\text{ among the first }N}P_E\\rho P_E$. [A1, A2, F6, F7, step 3.1]",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F7",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Each block $P_E\\rho P_E$ is positive trace class. The sum of their traces is $$\\sum_E\\sum_{u_j\\in\\operatorname{ran}P_E}\\langle\\rho u_j,u_j\\rangle =\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$$ by [F4] and the complete basis in [A1]. Therefore their finite partial sums are Cauchy in trace norm: for a positive finite block tail its trace norm equals its trace, and the scalar trace tail tends to zero. Their limit $\\rho_{\\rm diag}$ exists in trace norm, is positive, and has trace one. [A1, A2, F1, F4, step 4.1]",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Given $\\epsilon>0$, choose $N$ so the compression error in 2.1 and the diagonal tail in 3.2 are each below $\\epsilon/3$. The norm bound for the Riemann integral in 2.2 makes the average of the compression error at most $\\epsilon/3$, uniformly in $T$. For this fixed $N$, step 4.1 makes the finite compressed average differ from its finite diagonal limit by less than $\\epsilon/3$ for all sufficiently large $T$. The triangle inequality then gives $\\|\\bar\\rho_T-\\rho_{\\rm diag}\\|_1<\\epsilon$. [F2, step 2.2, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1]",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 4.1",
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "For bounded observable $B$, the trace-class ideal and trace estimates in [F2] and [F4] give $|\\operatorname{Tr}((\\bar\\rho_T-\\rho_{\\rm diag})B)|\\le\\|\\bar\\rho_T-\\rho_{\\rm diag}\\|_1\\|B\\|$. Step 6.1 sends the right-hand side to zero. The conclusion is only convergence of time-averaged states in the stated trace norm. [A2, F2, F4, step 6.1] ∎",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 6.1",
            "A2"
          ]
        }
      ],
