    "cex-tdmath-noncompact-mixture-nonattainment": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be nonempty compact, $d\\ge1$, and $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ continuous. Define $a_{\\rm mix}$ as in [[def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy]]. Then for every $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$ the infimum defining $a_{\\rm mix}(x)$ is attained by a Borel probability measure supported on at most $d+1$ points. The function $a_{\\rm mix}$ is lower semicontinuous and convex on $\\operatorname{conv}K$, and is the greatest convex function on $\\operatorname{conv}K$ bounded above by $a$ on $K$. Minimizing over all Borel probabilities on $K$ gives the same attained value as minimizing over finite mixtures. This is a mathematical compactness result; it makes no phase-preparation or equilibrium postulate.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each integer $n\\ge2$, the set $E_n=[1/n,1)$ is Borel, and $\\bigcup_{n\\ge2}E_n=(0,1)$. Since $P((0,1))=1$, countable subadditivity implies $P(E_n)>0$ for at least one $n$; otherwise the union would have probability zero. On $E_n$, $x\\ge1/n$, while elsewhere $x\\ge0$, so $$\\int_{(0,1)}x\\,dP\\ge \\frac1nP(E_n)>0.$$ Therefore every admissible probability has strictly positive objective. [A1, F1, algebra]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The point mass $\\delta_{1/n}$ at $1/n$ is a Borel probability on $(0,1)$ and has objective $1/n$. These values tend to zero, while 1.1 shows all objectives are positive. Hence the infimum is zero and is not attained. The generator set is noncompact, so this conclusion does not contradict the compact result [F2]; it shows why an attainment theorem must retain its compactness/coercivity hypotheses. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1] ∎",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "F1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The nonempty interval (0,1) is the specified generator set in A1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: proves every probability has strictly positive objective, while gives a sequence tending to zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The point masses delta_(1/n) explicitly form admissible one-point measures ."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No multiplicity or degenerate-generator case is part of this scalar example."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The excluded endpoint 0 is precisely why the infimum is not attained ."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The measures delta_(1/n) are explicit witnesses to the infimum ."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example states an unattained infimum, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example states an unattained infimum, not an iff."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "Point masses P=delta_x with x=1/n have energy x and tend to the unattained infimum 0.",
          "evidence": "Exact substitution in step 1.2."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md",
          "status": "Current draft supplier carrier; exact interface inspected; independent acceptance is not asserted.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.\n\n",
