    "cex-tdmath-nonergodic-volume-flow": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **symplectic form** on a smooth manifold $M$ is a smooth two-form $\\omega$\nsuch that\n\n1. $d\\omega=0$, and\n2. $(T_pM,\\omega_p)$ is a [[def-symplectic-vector-space|symplectic vector\n   space]] for every $p\\in M$.\n\nThe pair $(M,\\omega)$ is a **symplectic manifold**. Thus both closedness and\npointwise nondegeneracy are required. A zero-dimensional manifold with its\nzero two-form satisfies the definition.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-hamiltonian-vector-field-and-hamiltonian-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $H\\in C^\\infty(M)$, its **Hamiltonian vector field** is the vector field\n$X_H$ determined by the library sign convention\n\n$$\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH.$$\n\nA vector field $X$ is **Hamiltonian** if $\\iota_X\\omega=dH$ for some smooth\nfunction $H$; such an $H$ is a **Hamiltonian function for $X$**. A function and\nits vector field are distinct data, and completeness of $X_H$ is not assumed.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-hamiltonian-measure-and-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(M,\\omega)$ be a smooth $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold and $H\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, with Hamiltonian field defined by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. Wherever its local flow $\\phi_t$ is defined, it preserves the symplectic form and the volume measure $m$ determined by $\\omega^n/n!$. No completeness is asserted. For a region $R$ on which a nonzero-time map $T=\\phi_\\tau$ and all its iterates are defined, $T$ is a measure-preserving transformation. If $m(R)<\\infty$, then for every measurable $E\\subseteq R$, almost every point of $E$ returns to $E$ infinitely often under forward iteration.\n\nIf $m(R)=1$ and $T$ is ergodic, then for every $f\\in L^1(R,m)$ the averages $N^{-1}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}f(T^jx)$ converge almost everywhere and in $L^1$ to $\\int_Rf\\,dm$. Without ergodicity, the limit is the invariant conditional expectation supplied by Birkhoff's theorem, not necessarily the space mean. Finally, if each stated time map $\\phi_t$ is a diffeomorphism of a finite invariant region $R$, a density $p_0\\ge0$ with $\\int_Rp_0\\,dm=1$ and $\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty$ transports by $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$ with conserved fine-grained entropy $-k_B\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm$, using $0\\log0=0$. Volume preservation alone does not imply ergodicity or a time-average/ensemble identity.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure-preserving system is **ergodic for $\\mu$** if each $E\\in\\mathcal I$ has $\\mu(E)=0$ or $\\mu(X\\setminus E)=0$, with $\\mathcal I$ as in [[def-strict-and-mod-null-invariant-sigma-algebras]]. For a probability system this means $\\mu(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$. The definition is relative to the invariant measure; no probability assumption is implicit in the general null/conull formulation.\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $X_H=a\\partial_q+b\\partial_p$. Then $\\iota_{X_H}(dq\\wedge dp)=a\\,dp-b\\,dq$, while $dH=-\\sin q\\,dq$. Equating coefficients gives $a=0$ and $b=\\sin q$. The explicit solution is $$\\phi_t(q,p)=(q,p+t\\sin q\\pmod{2\\pi}),$$ which is defined for every real $t$ and is a diffeomorphism with inverse $\\phi_{-t}$. [A1, F1, algebra]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $H\\circ\\phi_t=H$, the set $A$ is invariant under every $\\phi_t$. The torus has total area $(2\\pi)^2=4\\pi^2$. The allowed $q$ values in $A$ have total length $\\pi$, while $p$ ranges over length $2\\pi$, so $m(A)=2\\pi^2$, strictly between zero and total area. The map preserves area by [F2], and the invariant set violates the criterion [F3]; hence it is not ergodic for any nonzero sampling time. [A1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The torus and invariant set A are nonempty by the coordinates and area calculation ."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The invariant set has positive area 2 pi squared ."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The two-dimensional symplectic torus is explicitly the minimal model in A1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Fixed points at sin q=0 are allowed and do not remove the positive-area invariant set ."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Angular boundary points are identified modulo 2 pi, and the flow is complete ."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The flow and invariant set are explicit."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example is a nonergodicity witness, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example is a nonergodicity witness, not an iff."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "The set cos(q)>0 has q-length pi and p-length 2 pi, area 2 pi squared; the full torus has area 4 pi squared.",
          "evidence": "Direct coordinate measure calculation in step 1.2."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
