    "ex-tdmath-pure-state-versus-average-entropy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-discrete-quantum-cesaro-dephasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the exact first-clause interface of [[thm-tdmath-general-trace-gibbs-model]]: a nonzero complex Hilbert space has a finite or countable complete orthonormal energy eigenbasis $(u_j)$ for a self-adjoint semibounded $A$, with each energy eigenspace finite-dimensional. The supplier is a current draft whose statement and complete argument have been inspected; this theorem is conditional on that interface and does not claim its independent acceptance. Let $\\hbar>0$, define $U_tu_j=e^{-iE_jt/\\hbar}u_j$, and extend linearly and continuously. For any positive trace-class density operator $\\rho$ with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, set $\\rho_t=U_t\\rho U_t^*$. If $P_E$ denotes the projection onto the full eigenspace of energy $E$, then the trace-norm Riemann average\n$$\\bar\\rho_T:=\\frac1T\\int_0^T\\rho_t\\,dt$$\nconverges as $T\\to\\infty$ to\n$$\\rho_{\\rm diag}:=\\sum_E P_E\\rho P_E$$\nin trace norm. The full degenerate energy blocks are retained. Thus expectations of every bounded observable converge to their diagonal-ensemble values. This asserts Cesàro convergence, not convergence of $\\rho_t$ at individual late times, and is restricted to the stated discrete trace-class spectrum.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, with **conjugation** $z\\mapsto\\overline z$\nthe identity on $\\mathbb R$ and complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). A **real** or\n**complex inner-product space** is an $\\mathbb F$-vector space $V$ together with\nan inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb F$ in the sense\nof [[def-inner-product-space]]: for all $u,v,w\\in V$ and $a,b\\in\\mathbb F$,\n\n$$\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle=a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle v,u\\rangle},\\qquad \\langle v,v\\rangle\\ge0,\\quad \\langle v,v\\rangle=0\\iff v=0 .$$\n\n**Conjugate-linearity in the second argument.** Conjugate symmetry together with\nlinearity in the first argument gives, for all $w\\in V$,\n\n$$\\langle w,au+bv\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle}=\\overline{a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle}=\\overline a\\,\\langle w,u\\rangle+\\overline b\\,\\langle w,v\\rangle .$$\n\n**Induced length.** The **induced length**, or inner-product norm, of $v$ is\n\n$$\\|v\\|:=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is exactly the published [[def-inner-product-norm]]: positive definiteness\nmakes $\\langle v,v\\rangle$ a nonnegative real with a unique nonnegative square\nroot, so that $\\|v\\|\\ge0$, $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly for $v=0$, and\n$\\langle v,v\\rangle=\\|v\\|^2$.\n\n**The convention on this page.** Every inner-product space below is real or\ncomplex in this sense, the pairing is linear in the first argument and\nconjugate-linear in the second, and $\\|v\\|$ always denotes the induced length.\nIn the real case conjugation is the identity and $|z|$ is the absolute value of\n$\\mathbb R$; in the complex case they are complex conjugation and the complex\nmodulus. All statements below are therefore written once and read in either\nscalar field.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The unitary evolution sends $\\psi$ to $(e^{-iE_1t/\\hbar}u_1+e^{-iE_2t/\\hbar}u_2)/\\sqrt2$, which remains a unit vector by [F2]. Its rank-one density has eigenvalues $1,0$, so its entropy is $-k_B(1\\log1+0\\log0)=0$ for every $t$. [A1, F2, algebra]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The two energy eigenspaces are one-dimensional. The dephasing theorem [F1] removes their off-diagonal matrix entries and yields $\\rho_{\\rm diag}=\\tfrac12P_1+\\tfrac12P_2$. Its eigenvalues are $(1/2,1/2)$, so $$S(\\rho_{\\rm diag})=-k_B\\left(\\tfrac12\\log\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\log\\tfrac12\\right)=k_B\\log2.$$ Since every $S(\\rho_t)=0$, the time average of instantaneous entropy is exactly zero. [A1, F1, F3, step 1.1]",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the basis $(u_1,u_2)$, $\\bar\\rho_T$ has diagonal entries $1/2$ and off-diagonal entry $$c_T=\\frac1{2T}\\int_0^T e^{-i(E_1-E_2)t/\\hbar}\\,dt,$$ whose magnitude is at most $\\hbar/(T|E_1-E_2|)$ by direct integration. Its eigenvalues are $1/2\\pm|c_T|$, so both tend to $1/2$. Continuity of $x\\mapsto-x\\log x$ at $1/2$ gives $S(\\bar\\rho_T)\\to k_B\\log2$. Thus entropy of the averaged state has a nonzero limit even though the averaged instantaneous entropy remains zero. [A1, F2, F3, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example uses the nonzero two-dimensional Hilbert space C^2."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The pure-state zero entropy and 0 log 0 convention are evaluated ."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Each pure state has one nonzero eigenvalue, computed ."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: Distinct energies are assumed; the dephasing result retains one-dimensional energy blocks ."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The finite T average is explicitly defined and its T to infinity entropy limit is evaluated ."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The vectors and density matrix are explicitly specified."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example makes no iff claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example makes no iff claim."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "The density eigenvalues change from (1,0) instantaneously to the dephased limit (1/2,1/2); the entropies are 0 and k_B log 2.",
          "evidence": "Direct eigenvalue and entropy calculations in steps 1.1–1.2."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
